6082528a-b0e7-4549-ab82-96f0aa98be91	dfb973ca-17ab-455d-8f1d-ab2bf37d64f9	research-003	00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000018	research	research	Review existing evidence and model knowledge to support the illustrative energy requirement calculation.	A summary of relevant model knowledge and assumptions used in the energy requirement calculation.	{bb4fdc9d-a8a9-48f8-8660-c5276abbbd72}	completed	1	00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000206	60babb75-9461-41af-9a5b-b7a6f34ce2d1	\N	Model knowledge indicates that energy requirements for a given task or system are typically calculated based on factors such as power consumption, operational duration, efficiency, and environmental conditions. Assumptions commonly used in such calculations include:\n\n1. **Power Consumption**: The energy requirement is often based on the rated power consumption of the system or device, assuming it operates at full capacity unless otherwise specified.\n2. **Operational Duration**: The time period over which the system operates is a key factor in determining total energy use.\n3. **Efficiency**: Systems may not operate at 100% efficiency, so energy losses due to inefficiencies are often accounted for in the calculation.\n4. **Environmental Conditions**: Temperature, humidity, and other environmental factors can influence energy consumption, particularly in systems with cooling or heating requirements.\n5. **Load Variations**: If the system experiences varying loads, the calculation may assume an average load or use a worst-case scenario based on typical usage patterns.\n\nThese assumptions and model knowledge are used to estimate energy requirements for illustrative purposes, ensuring that the calculation reflects real-world operational conditions.\n\nEvidence status: model knowledge only. No external sources were consulted, and citations were not verified.	2026-11-01 01:55:14.068-04	\N	\N	2026-11-01 01:45:11.767742-04	2026-11-01 01:45:19.877-04	2026-11-01 01:45:14.3-04	2026-11-01 01:45:19.877-04
75e27292-fea0-486f-b003-9a61f7c2d62f	dfb973ca-17ab-455d-8f1d-ab2bf37d64f9	science-002	00000000-0000-4000-8000-00000000001b	science	science	Evaluate engineering tradeoffs for energy generation and storage systems suitable for a remote research station.	A list of engineering tradeoffs, including system efficiency, reliability, and maintenance requirements.	{bb4fdc9d-a8a9-48f8-8660-c5276abbbd72}	completed	1	00000000-0000-4000-8000-000000000205	5a43c9f4-330c-4467-b9c0-c4ce507835e7	\N	- **System Efficiency**: Higher efficiency systems may require more complex technology or higher initial costs, which could be challenging in remote locations with limited resources.  \n- **Reliability**: More reliable systems often involve greater redundancy or more robust components, which can increase weight, cost, and complexity.  \n- **Maintenance Requirements**: Systems with lower maintenance needs may use less durable or less efficient components, potentially leading to more frequent failures or downtime.  \n- **Energy Source Compatibility**: Choosing between renewable (e.g., solar, wind) and non-renewable (e.g., diesel generators) sources involves tradeoffs in sustainability, fuel logistics, and environmental impact.  \n- **Storage Capacity vs. Weight/Space**: Larger energy storage systems (e.g., batteries) provide more energy availability but require more physical space and weight, which may be constrained in remote stations.  \n- **Scalability**: Systems designed for future expansion may require more initial investment and infrastructure, which may not be feasible in a remote setting with limited budget.  \n- **Environmental Impact**: Some energy systems may have a greater ecological footprint, which is a critical consideration for remote research stations in sensitive environments.	2026-11-01 01:55:20.153-04	\N	\N	2026-11-01 01:45:11.767742-04	2026-11-01 01:45:26.591-04	2026-11-01 01:45:20.424-04	2026-11-01 01:45:26.591-04
